== stz_sound: the portable sample tier ==

  stz_sound.dll loaded; IsAvailable = 1  (always 1 -- it needs no hardware)

-- Scene 1: buffer ids are GENERATION-KEYED, so a freed id is detected --
   A recycled slot must not answer to the dead id. The counter is the
   witness: sound.stale.hits moves on the dead read and NOT on the live one.
  [ok]   a new buffer has a non-zero id
  [ok]   frames reads back
  [ok]   channels reads back
  [ok]   rate reads back
  [ok]   a LIVE read does not count as stale
  [ok]   free succeeds once
  [ok]   freeing twice returns STALE, not OK
  [ok]   a stale read returns -1, not 0
  [ok]   sound.stale.hits MOVED
  [ok]   the new buffer got a different id
  [ok]   the new buffer reads its own length
  [ok]   the OLD id still reads -1 after the slot was recycled

-- Scene 2: silence is silent, set/get is exact, out of range is COUNTED --
  [ok]   a silent buffer peaks at exactly 0
  [ok]   and its rms is exactly 0
  [ok]   a written sample reads back exactly
  [ok]   the OTHER channel of that frame is untouched
  [ok]   peak now sees it
  [ok]   writing past the end returns BAD_ARG
  [ok]   sound.refusals MOVED
  [ok]   and the buffer was NOT changed by the refused write

-- Scene 3: a WAV round-trip returns the SAMPLES, not something like them --
   f32 must be bit-exact. s16 must NOT be -- and asserting that is what
   proves the comparison would notice a real corruption.
  [ok]   saving 32-bit WAV succeeds
  [ok]   saving 16-bit WAV succeeds
  [ok]   the 32-bit file decodes
  [ok]   frames survive
  [ok]   rate survives
  [ok]   channels survive
  [ok]   f32 round-trip is BIT-EXACT
  [ok]   the 16-bit file decodes
   worst s16 round-trip deviation: 0.000041782856   (one quantum is 0.000030517578)
  [ok]   s16 round-trip is CLOSE (within a quantum)
  [ok]   s16 round-trip is NOT exact -- the comparison has teeth

-- Scene 4: an ARABIC filename survives encode AND decode --
   miniaudio's narrow path reaches fopen, which on Windows reads bytes in
   the ANSI codepage: a UTF-8 Arabic name fails to open a file that plainly
   exists. The engine converts to UTF-16 and uses the _w variants. The
   filename is not printed -- Windows consoles garble it -- but it is used.
  [ok]   saving to an Arabic-named path succeeds
  [ok]   and the file really exists on disk under that name
  [ok]   the Arabic-named file decodes
  [ok]   its samples are bit-exact
  [ok]   a genuinely missing file returns 0
  [ok]   and LastError says something

-- Scene 5: resampling keeps duration and DC gain, and sinc beats linear --
   Both resamplers are OURS, in Zig (plan sec.1). The assertion is against
   an ANALYTIC reference sine, not against the other resampler, so 'better'
   means closer to the truth rather than merely different.
  [ok]   the sinc resample produced a buffer
  [ok]   the linear resample produced a buffer
  [ok]   the new rate is 44100
  [ok]   duration is preserved within one frame
  [ok]   the source buffer was NOT mutated
   max error vs the analytic 1 kHz sine:  sinc 0.000021909055   linear 0.002133933107
  [ok]   sinc tracks the true sine closely
  [ok]   linear is measurably worse -- the metric discriminates
   worst DC deviation across ALL 1000 upsampled frames: 0
  [ok]   DC gain is unity everywhere, edges included

-- Scene 6: channel conversion averages down, replicates up, mutates nothing --
  [ok]   stereo -> mono gives 1 channel
  [ok]   and it is the AVERAGE, not the first channel
  [ok]   mono -> stereo gives 2 channels
  [ok]   left carries the mono signal
  [ok]   right carries the same signal
  [ok]   the ORIGINAL stereo buffer is untouched
  [ok]   frames are preserved by conversion

